WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.895
In Einstein's theory of gravity,

2
00:00:05.895 --> 00:00:10.215
a wormhole is a tunnel through space with two openings, called mouths.

3
00:00:10.653 --> 00:00:14.045
A signal can go from one mouth to the other in two ways:

4
00:00:14.045 --> 00:00:18.467
through the tunnel, called the throat, or through ordinary space outside.

5
00:00:18.887 --> 00:00:20.170
For a signal to pass,

6
00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:24.083
something must hold the throat open. In the model of this video,

7
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:25.795
quantum fields hold it open.

8
00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.965
For three decades, physicists have argued about one question:

9
00:00:29.965 --> 00:00:34.140
do quantum fields stop such a wormhole from becoming a time machine?

10
00:00:34.587 --> 00:00:37.845
A 2026 paper takes one model of such a wormhole,

11
00:00:37.845 --> 00:00:42.666
pushes the clocks of its two mouths apart, and asks what the fields do.

12
00:00:43.087 --> 00:00:45.790
The paper does not say whether time travel is

13
00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:49.035
possible. Everything in this video is about one model.

14
00:00:50.553 --> 00:00:53.247
Each mouth has its own clock. Suppose that

15
00:00:53.247 --> 00:00:56.710
mouth 1 sits deeper in a well of gravity than mouth 2.

16
00:00:57.153 --> 00:01:02.407
Then the clock of mouth 1 runs slower. The paper calls this difference the drive, δ.

17
00:01:02.853 --> 00:01:10.524
The clock of mouth 1 runs at e^−δ times the rate of mouth 2. With δ = 1,

18
00:01:10.524 --> 00:01:12.548
that is about 0.37.

19
00:01:12.987 --> 00:01:17.208
So a time shift builds up between the two clocks. If nothing pushed back,

20
00:01:17.208 --> 00:01:19.463
it would keep growing at a steady rate.

21
00:01:20.287 --> 00:01:21.085
In this model,

22
00:01:21.085 --> 00:01:23.422
the throat is long. Light takes more time

23
00:01:23.422 --> 00:01:25.931
through the throat than through the outside.

24
00:01:26.353 --> 00:01:28.785
The paper watches two margins. Margin A:

25
00:01:28.785 --> 00:01:33.223
how much later light arrives through the throat than through the outside,

26
00:01:33.223 --> 00:01:35.594
as the clocks of the mouths measure it.

27
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.172
Margin B: how long light takes to go once around,

28
00:01:39.172 --> 00:01:40.588
through the throat and

29
00:01:40.588 --> 00:01:44.962
back outside. A time shift makes this loop shorter in one direction.

30
00:01:45.387 --> 00:01:47.970
If A falls to zero, or B goes to zero,

31
00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:51.233
a path of light could close on itself. The paper

32
00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:55.040
calls this the model's threshold for closed null curves.

33
00:01:56.587 --> 00:02:02.329
The paper uses a wormhole that Maldacena, Milekhin and Popov described in 2018.

34
00:02:02.753 --> 00:02:06.329
Two black holes with opposite magnetic charges sit at

35
00:02:06.329 --> 00:02:10.174
a distance d. The regions near them join into one throat.

36
00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.427
Charged particles called fermions move along

37
00:02:13.427 --> 00:02:16.425
the magnetic field lines. The lines form loops,

38
00:02:16.425 --> 00:02:18.721
through the throat and back outside.

39
00:02:19.153 --> 00:02:23.464
In a closed loop, quantum fields carry a small negative energy,

40
00:02:23.464 --> 00:02:27.775
called Casimir energy. Here, this energy holds the throat open.

41
00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.923
So the fields that hold the wormhole open are the

42
00:02:30.923 --> 00:02:33.902
same fields that feel a time shift between the mouths.

43
00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.545
The paper describes the throat with a simple two-dimensional theory of gravity,

44
00:02:39.545 --> 00:02:43.911
at linear order. It uses two models of the loop, called R1 and R2.

45
00:02:44.353 --> 00:02:46.766
The paper does not model how the drive is

46
00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:49.356
produced. The drive is switched on smoothly,

47
00:02:49.356 --> 00:02:51.827
and in some runs it is switched off again.

48
00:02:53.353 --> 00:02:55.553
First, the paper looks at a slow limit,

49
00:02:55.553 --> 00:02:58.147
in which the fields keep up with every change.

50
00:02:58.587 --> 00:02:59.629
With a time shift,

51
00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:02.291
fermions that go one way around the loop see a

52
00:03:02.291 --> 00:03:05.765
shorter loop. Those that go the other way see a longer loop.

53
00:03:06.187 --> 00:03:08.846
Their Casimir energies are then different. The

54
00:03:08.846 --> 00:03:12.141
difference is a steady flow of energy through the throat.

55
00:03:12.587 --> 00:03:15.388
This flow moves mass from one mouth to the other.

56
00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.184
The change in mass changes the clock rates of the mouths,

57
00:03:19.184 --> 00:03:21.545
and this change works against the drive.

58
00:03:21.987 --> 00:03:24.611
There is one point of balance. There,

59
00:03:24.611 --> 00:03:29.009
the clock ratio of the throat, e^δ, cancels the drive exactly.

60
00:03:30.553 --> 00:03:35.252
But the system does not settle at this point. It swings around it, again and again.

61
00:03:35.687 --> 00:03:40.212
Compare a ball that rolls in a bowl without friction. It passes the bottom,

62
00:03:40.212 --> 00:03:41.842
and it does not stop there.

63
00:03:42.287 --> 00:03:42.989
The reason:

64
00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:47.652
the slow equations do not change when time runs backward. Near the point,

65
00:03:47.652 --> 00:03:50.974
they give a pure swing, with no growth and no decay.

66
00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.257
When the drive is switched off,

67
00:03:53.257 --> 00:03:56.398
the system keeps moving on a closed path. In general,

68
00:03:56.398 --> 00:03:58.709
it does not return to where it started.

69
00:03:59.153 --> 00:04:02.143
One caution: this limit needs slow changes,

70
00:04:02.143 --> 00:04:04.715
but the swing itself is not slow. The

71
00:04:04.715 --> 00:04:08.052
paper uses the limit only to find the mechanism.

72
00:04:09.587 --> 00:04:13.195
Next, the paper adds the time that light needs to cross the loop.

73
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.917
A small disturbance can be split into modes. Each mode has a number

74
00:04:17.917 --> 00:04:22.277
called a root. If a root lies off the vertical line in this picture,

75
00:04:22.277 --> 00:04:24.073
its mode grows or dies away.

76
00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.126
In each of the 12 cases,

77
00:04:26.126 --> 00:04:29.537
the 28 roots inside the counting region lie exactly

78
00:04:29.537 --> 00:04:33.217
on the line. Three of them come from the method itself.

79
00:04:33.653 --> 00:04:36.628
Exact checks, including interval arithmetic,

80
00:04:36.628 --> 00:04:39.940
confirm this. So no counted mode decays or grows.

81
00:04:40.387 --> 00:04:41.783
After the drive stops,

82
00:04:41.783 --> 00:04:45.782
the slow swing turns into a root whose pattern matches a boost:

83
00:04:45.782 --> 00:04:48.321
a change of viewpoint inside the throat.

84
00:04:48.753 --> 00:04:53.874
In a related theory, such changes are not physical. The paper does not settle this,

85
00:04:53.874 --> 00:04:57.266
so it does not claim the slow swing as a physical mode.

86
00:04:58.187 --> 00:05:01.852
Then the full equations are run,

87
00:05:01.852 --> 00:05:08.037
for drives of 0.001, 0.01 and 0.1. That makes 18 runs.

88
00:05:08.487 --> 00:05:13.791
For 50 swings after the drive is switched on, or off, both margins stay positive.

89
00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:18.265
The time shift reaches at most 0.4 to 0.5 times δ,

90
00:05:18.265 --> 00:05:24.576
while the drive is switched on or off. After the drive stops, a swing remains.

91
00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.292
For the two smallest drives, this says little. Even with no push back,

92
00:05:29.292 --> 00:05:34.053
the shift would not reach the threshold in the same time, in all but one case.

93
00:05:35.587 --> 00:05:44.166
The paper then raises the drive to 0.2, 0.3, 0.6 and 1. That is 48 more runs.

94
00:05:44.587 --> 00:05:47.400
In the windows fixed in advance,

95
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:54.259
both margins stay positive. The smallest values are 1.16 for A and 2.03 for B.

96
00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.038
So, within these runs,

97
00:05:56.038 --> 00:05:59.848
the model does not reach its threshold for closed null curves.

98
00:06:00.287 --> 00:06:06.855
The largest time shift grows with the drive. At δ = 1, it is 0.421.

99
00:06:07.287 --> 00:06:08.756
Two runs stopped early,

100
00:06:08.756 --> 00:06:11.503
at a numerical limit of the time steps. The

101
00:06:11.503 --> 00:06:14.506
paper says nothing about them after that point.

102
00:06:16.053 --> 00:06:19.392
A model like this is valid only while some

103
00:06:19.392 --> 00:06:22.890
ratios stay small. The paper sets two lines:

104
00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.990
0.1 or less holds, and 1 or more fails.

105
00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:28.160
These lines are conventions,

106
00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.076
fixed before the computation. They are not physical thresholds.

107
00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:36.793
At δ = 1, one ratio, S8, reaches 1 in five of six cases,

108
00:06:36.793 --> 00:06:39.616
before the margins fail. Taken alone,

109
00:06:39.616 --> 00:06:43.278
this would say that the description breaks down.

110
00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:47.971
But S8 depends on how the points of the throat are labelled. A boost,

111
00:06:47.971 --> 00:06:50.251
a change of viewpoint, can change it.

112
00:06:50.687 --> 00:06:53.807
So the paper builds new ratios from what each mouth

113
00:06:53.807 --> 00:06:57.355
measures with its own clock. A boost does not change them.

114
00:06:57.787 --> 00:07:05.125
With these ratios, the largest value at δ = 1 is 0.666. That is below 1.

115
00:07:05.553 --> 00:07:10.161
With these ratios, a value of 1 comes only from two fixed ratios,

116
00:07:10.161 --> 00:07:13.918
S2 and S3. They are set by the number of fermions and

117
00:07:13.918 --> 00:07:16.115
the coupling, before any drive.

118
00:07:16.553 --> 00:07:20.129
At the larger drives, most cases are undecided,

119
00:07:20.129 --> 00:07:23.019
inside the band from 0.1 to 1. No case

120
00:07:23.019 --> 00:07:26.670
reached the verdicts “stops” or “does not stop”.

121
00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:30.982
The paper does not say whether time travel is possible,

122
00:07:30.982 --> 00:07:34.185
or whether the chronology protection conjecture holds.

123
00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.732
All its statements hold in one model, for the loops,

124
00:07:37.732 --> 00:07:40.245
drives and run windows that were computed.

125
00:07:40.687 --> 00:07:42.781
Some effects are not in the model,

126
00:07:42.781 --> 00:07:46.537
for example radiation from the mouths. They could take energy

127
00:07:46.537 --> 00:07:50.787
away from the swing. The lack of damping belongs to the closed model.

128
00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:56.576
The lines 0.1 and 1 are conventions. And the new ratios are one choice,

129
00:07:56.576 --> 00:07:58.462
not the only correct one.

130
00:07:58.887 --> 00:08:01.990
To read the paper, look up this DOI. The paper,

131
00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:04.565
the code and the records are on Zenodo.

132
00:08:04.987 --> 00:08:08.888
This video was made with the help of AI. The voice is synthetic.
